course: dictionary
lesson: 2973
title: Drunk: a verb and a describing word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word «drunk» has two jobs. Its first job is as part of the verb «drink»: drink, drank, drunk. «I have drunk my milk» uses it this way, and the helper word «have» comes just before it.
explain: Its second job is to describe a grown-up who has had too much alcohol and cannot think, speak or move properly. Alcohol is never for children. A drunk person may be unsteady on their feet and may act strangely, so keep away and tell a trusted adult if you feel unsafe.
explain: Look at the words around it to find its job. «Cleaning products such as bleach must never be drunk, even a little, because they burn the throat.» Here «drunk» is part of a verb about swallowing, not a description of a person. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it.
fact: «Drunk» is the past participle of «drink»: drink, drank, drunk.
fact: «I drank my juice yesterday» uses drank, and «I have drunk my juice» uses drunk.
fact: «Drunk» can also describe a person who has had too much alcohol and cannot think, speak or move properly.
fact: If someone who has been drinking alcohol wants to drive you, never get in the car. Tell a trusted adult straight away.
fact: Long ago in Mexico and Central America, cocoa was drunk bitter and frothy, and the Aztecs even used cocoa beans as money.
exercise: «I have drunk my milk.» In this sentence, what is «drunk» doing? | It is a name for a kind of cup | *It is part of the verb drink, past form | It is a place name | It describes a grown-up who cannot walk
exercise: «The man was so drunk that he could not walk straight.» What does drunk mean here? | Very happy and singing | Cold and wet | Thirsty after a long run | *Had too much alcohol and could not move properly
exercise: «Cleaning products such as bleach must never be drunk.» Why must they never be drunk? | *Because they can burn the throat | Because they taste nice | Because they are thick like soup | Because they are cold
test: The past participle of drink, used after «have», is: | *drunk | drinks | drank | drinking
test: Which word means a person who has had too much alcohol and cannot think or move properly? | Thirsty | *Drunk | Sleepy | Curt
exam: Fill the gap: «Yesterday I drank my juice, and today I have ___ my juice.» | *drunk | drinks | drink | drank
